## 3. Find and prepare your Philippine fish dataset

One dataset this time — there's no separate detection dataset to build.

3.1. Identify target species — prioritize ones with a closed season, size limit, or protected status (matches Section 6.2's species list: Tamban, Alumahan, Hasa-hasa, 
     Galunggong, Danggit, Lapu-lapu, etc., plus common aquaculture species like Bangus and Tilapia if you want easier wins)
3.2. Source images from Kaggle (the manuscript's Section 3.2 names Kaggle specifically as the dataset source and scopes the model to whatever species it contains), 
     supplemented by BFAR references or your own market photography if needed — no identifiable faces in your own photos
3.3. Build a single labeled dataset: full, uncropped images with a species label per image — no bounding-box annotation needed, since there's no detection stage 
     (Section 7.3: the whole image is resized and normalized directly as CNN input)
3.4. Capture images the way an end-user would: fish clearly visible and reasonably centered as the primary subject (Section 3.3's assumption — 
     the system does not perform automatic fish detection or isolation, so training data should reflect the same framing users are expected to provide)
3.5. Organize the dataset into folders by species name (`/ml/dataset/`)
3.6. Build the master CSV/JSON for `FishSpecies` attributes: `commonName`, `scientificName`, `localNames`, `description`, `habitat`, `averageSize`, `conservationStatus`, 
     `minimumSize`, `defaultFishingRestrictions`

---

## 4. Train and export the CNN classifier

4.1. Choose architecture: Pick a lightweight architecture suited to mobile: MobileNetV3 or EfficientNet-Lite are good starting points 
     — they're built for exactly this kind of on-device constraint (matches the model comparison table in Section 10.2)
4.2. Train: Train on the labeled dataset from Step 3 to classify species directly from the full image (no cropping — Section 7.3). 
     Evaluate with Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score, and Top-3 Accuracy (Section 10.1.1) 
     - Top-3 matters because the app shows ranked alternative candidates (MLR-007). 
     Also check the recall-per-species target: at least 80% recall for every species with a closed season, size limit, or protected status (Section 5.2), not just overall accuracy.
4.3. Export: Export to TFLite (`model.export(format="tflite")`). Apply INT8 quantization for speed and size on budget phones. Save the exported file as `species_classifier.tflite`.
4.4. Generate the class-index file: `labels.txt` maps the CNN's output indices to species names — this is what MLR-014 refers to when it says predictions 
     must be traceable to the specific trained artifact. Version this file alongside the model; a mismatch here is a silent, dangerous bug (confident predictions with wrong names).
4.5. Sanity-check before touching Android: Run a test script on your machine: image in → resize/normalize → CNN classifies → prints species, confidence, and top-3 alternatives. 
     Confirm this works end-to-end in Python before wiring it into Kotlin.

